  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p><strong>Statement I</strong> : Rotation of the earth shows effect on the value of acceleration due to gravity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.</p><p><strong>Statement II</strong> : The effect of rotation of the earth on the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math>&nbsp;at the equator is minimum and that at the pole is maximum.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "kuRiO",
      "text": "Statement I is false but statement II is true",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "kHgcT",
      "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "uUCBu",
      "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "gGVbF",
      "text": "Statement I is true but statement II is false",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The variation of acceleration due to gravity with the rotation of Earth can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>&#39;</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Substitute&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>&nbsp;for equatorial position to obtain the acceleration due to gravity.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>g</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>g</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Substitute&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi></math>&nbsp;for polar position to obtain the acceleration due to gravity.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>g</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>g</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>g</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>The above two equations show that the effect on acceleration due to gravity&nbsp;is maximum at the equator and minimum at pole.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
